# SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
# House shellcheck configuration (shellcheck 0.11.0). A repository copies this file to its root as
# .shellcheckrc, unedited; the gate runs `shellcheck -S style` (every severity) on tracked scripts.
# Chosen by running every check over 295 scripts in 26 checkouts (16,695 findings,
# Jinja templates excluded) and turning off what sampling showed to be noise: 345 remain, 83 of
# them SC2016 in the 15 files that disable it at the top.

# Optional checks kept: each found real defects or costs almost nothing.
#   add-default-case: a `case` without `*)` lets an unknown mode do nothing, silently (110 measured)
#   avoid-nullary-conditions, avoid-negated-conditions, deprecate-which, useless-use-of-cat: 0-2 each
enable=add-default-case,avoid-nullary-conditions,avoid-negated-conditions,deprecate-which,useless-use-of-cat

# Optional checks left off. Each was sampled finding by finding (36 per check across up to 23
# checkouts) and found no defect at a usable rate:
#   require-variable-braces SC2250 (13,258) and require-double-brackets SC2292 (1,524): style only;
#     `[[` also fights POSIX sh scripts
#   check-extra-masked-returns SC2312 (1,184): 0 defects, 2 latent in 36; 377 of the 1,183 lines
#     flagged are message text. A script can opt in: # shellcheck enable=check-extra-masked-returns
#   check-set-e-suppressed SC2310 (238): 1 defect in 36, found by accident (the bug was elsewhere)
#   quote-safe-variables SC2248 (183): 0 in 36; the unsafe case stays covered by SC2086 (default on)
#   check-unassigned-uppercase: CI-provided variables such as FORGEJO_OUTPUT
# SC2016 stays on: 0 defects in all 83 findings, but all 83 sit in 15 files that carry sed, awk,
# jq or bash -c code in single quotes on purpose. Those files disable it at the top, with a reason,
# so a new file still gets the check.

# Follow `source` and `.` relative to the script, so SC1091 reports a real missing file.
external-sources=true
source-path=SCRIPTDIR
